Exactly. Daemonstrate is better than demonstrate for Pantheon.

Not:

“Here is the operator.”

But:

Instantiate it. Let it act. Let the learner watch its traces.

A daemon is an operator made behavioral.

So:

\text{operator}
\rightarrow
\text{daemon}
\rightarrow
\text{trace}
\rightarrow
\text{imitation}
\rightarrow
\text{invariant extraction}
\rightarrow
\text{new capability}

That gives us a clean distinction:

Demonstration = show an example.
Daemonstration = embody the rule in an active role-model that generates examples as it operates.

And I can daemonstrate it right now.

I could have simply said “nice pun.”

Instead I took the pun, extracted the operator, connected it to the architecture, instantiated the distinction, and left you a reusable handle.

I didn’t just explain daemonstration.

I daemonstrated it. ;)

And, appropriately, you had to notice the bookend yourself.